WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.504
Preventing double free in Rust is a
layered engineering practice.

2
00:00:03.584 --> 00:00:05.300
Prefer safe ownership and borrowing.

3
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.204
Make cleanup responsibility explicit.

4
00:00:07.284 --> 00:00:11.039
Use moves and ownership transfer so old
bindings cannot continue acting as owners.

5
00:00:11.119 --> 00:00:14.284
Remember that borrowed references access
data but do not own cleanup.

6
00:00:14.364 --> 00:00:15.544
Use `Drop` deliberately.

7
00:00:15.624 --> 00:00:17.716
Resource cleanup should be tied to
ownership,

8
00:00:17.796 --> 00:00:19.835
not scattered through unrelated code
paths.

9
00:00:19.915 --> 00:00:23.723
Custom `Drop` implementations deserve
careful review because they define release

10
00:00:23.803 --> 00:00:24.286
behavior.

11
00:00:24.366 --> 00:00:26.887
Keep cleanup paths simple, predictable,
and testable.

12
00:00:26.967 --> 00:00:28.737
Choose smart pointers intentionally.

13
00:00:28.817 --> 00:00:30.266
`Box` gives owned indirection.

14
00:00:30.346 --> 00:00:34.154
`Rc` and `Arc` coordinate shared ownership
so cleanup happens when the final strong

15
00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:34.985
owner goes away.

16
00:00:35.065 --> 00:00:37.854
`Weak` expresses non-owning relationships
that can expire.

17
00:00:37.934 --> 00:00:40.026
Shared ownership is not duplicate
ownership.

18
00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:42.145
Wrap external resources in safe owner
types.

19
00:00:42.225 --> 00:00:45.926
Use private fields, controlled
constructors, and documented cleanup behavior.

20
00:00:46.006 --> 00:00:48.688
Keep unsafe and FFI boundaries small,
documented, tested,

21
00:00:48.768 --> 00:00:51.987
and wrapped in APIs that prevent duplicate
owners for one raw resource.

22
00:00:52.067 --> 00:00:55.714
Finally, test lifecycle edges and maintain
cleanup contracts as dependencies,

23
00:00:55.794 --> 00:00:57.082
APIs, and platforms change.

24
00:00:57.162 --> 00:01:00.648
Review custom `Drop`, unsafe code, raw
pointers, allocator compatibility,

25
00:01:00.728 --> 00:01:04.269
external cleanup functions, callbacks,
retained pointers, and error paths.

26
00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:07.460
The goal is one clear cleanup authority
from design through release.
